## The definition

A controlled component is a form input whose displayed value is owned by React state. You wire two things: the `value` prop reads from state, and `onChange` writes back to it.

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  return (
    <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />
  );
}
```

React is the single source of truth — what the user sees is always whatever state says.

## Why it is useful

Because every keystroke passes through your handler, you can react to input as it happens: validate, format (uppercase, mask a phone number), enforce a max length, enable or disable a submit button, or mirror the value elsewhere.

## The cost

Each keystroke is a state update and a re-render. For ordinary forms that is unnoticeable; for a very large form you might reach for an uncontrolled approach or a form library.

## Contrast with uncontrolled

An uncontrolled input keeps its value in the DOM; you give it `defaultValue` and read it with a ref. Controlled is the usual default — the data stays in one predictable place. Note: passing `value` without `onChange` makes a read-only field React will warn about.

detailMdTr:
## Tanım

Controlled component, görüntülenen değeri React state'inin sahip olduğu bir form input'udur. İki şeyi bağlarsın: `value` prop'u state'ten okur ve `onChange` geri yazar.

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  return (
    <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />
  );
}
```

React tek doğruluk kaynağıdır — kullanıcının gördüğü her zaman state'in söylediğidir.

## Neden Kullanışlı

Her tuş basımı handler'ından geçtiğinden, giriş olurken tepki verebilirsin: doğrula, biçimlendir, maksimum uzunluk uygula, gönder butonunu etkinleştir ya da devre dışı bırak.

## Maliyet

Her tuş basımı bir state güncellemesi ve bir yeniden render'dır. Olağan formlar için fark edilmez; çok büyük bir form için uncontrolled yaklaşıma ya da bir form kütüphanesine yönelebilirsin.

## Uncontrolled ile Karşılaştırma

Uncontrolled input değerini DOM'da tutar; ona `defaultValue` verirsin ve bir ref ile okursun. Controlled olağan varsayılandır — veri bir, öngörülebilir yerde durur.

detailMd:
## In function components: useRef

```jsx
function TextField() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function focusIt() {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }

  return (
    <>
      <input ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={focusIt}>Focus</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

`useRef(initial)` returns an object whose `.current` React keeps stable across renders. Attach it with the `ref` attribute and, once mounted, `.current` is the DOM node.

## In class components: createRef

```jsx
class TextField extends React.Component {
  inputRef = React.createRef();
  render() { return <input ref={this.inputRef} />; }
}
```

`createRef` is the class-era equivalent — created once as an instance field.

## Callback refs

Instead of a ref object you can pass a function. React calls it with the node when it attaches and with `null` when it detaches — useful for running logic at exactly that moment, or for measuring a node.

## Reaching a child component

A parent cannot ref a function child's DOM directly. The child either accepts a ref as a normal prop (React 19) or is wrapped in `forwardRef` (earlier versions) to forward it onto an element.

